# sports-fitness.kb - Sports / Fitness
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 5 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.

cardio | definition | Cardio is any exercise that raises heart rate for a sustained period, and it is the category most people mean when they say they need to get fit. The measure that matters is not the heart rate at its peak but how much of the week is spent in a range where talking becomes difficult. That range is the one that changes fitness, and it is lower than most beginners assume, which is why easy sessions do more than they appear to. | cardio, heart rate, sustained, fitness, range, easy | what is cardio, is cardio the same as aerobic, how often should i do cardio | cardio | cardio
progressive overload | definition | Progressive overload is the principle that a muscle adapts only when it is asked to do more than it currently can, and it applies to almost every kind of training rather than just weightlifting. The extra can be weight, repetitions, distance, or difficulty, and it has to be small and gradual. Doing too much too fast is what produces injury rather than progress, since tendons and connective tissue adapt more slowly than muscle does. | progressive overload, adapt, muscle, weight, repetition, tendon | what is progressive overload, how do you apply it, is it only for weights | progressive overload | progressive overload
mobility | definition | Mobility is the range a joint can move through, and it is separate from flexibility, which is the length of a muscle at rest. Strengthening a range that a joint cannot actually use is the usual mistake, since a heavy squat through a restricted ankle does not train the squat, it trains compensation. The practical work is moving each joint through its full range unloaded and slowly, which most people find uncomfortable and therefore skip, and that discomfort is the useful signal. | mobility, joint, range, flexibility, loaded, compensate | what is mobility, difference between mobility and flexibility, how to improve mobility | mobility | mobility
delOAD | definition | Deload is a week of reduced training volume, and it is the least popular part of a programme because it feels like going backwards while the training log still looks empty. The reason it works is that fitness and fatigue are separate: a hard week builds both, and the fitness outlasts the fatigue while the fatigue does not. Cutting the volume for a week while keeping the intensity lets the fatigue clear without losing the adaptation. | deload, volume, fatigue, week, intensity, recover | what is a deload week, is deloading necessary, when should you deload | deload | deload
warm-up | definition | A warm-up raises temperature and primes the movement pattern before the main effort, and it is not the same as stretching, which is a separate and much more debated activity. What actually helps is moving through the specific actions about to be performed, lightly at first, so the nervous system has run the pattern once. Static stretching held for a long time before a sprint can temporarily reduce force output, which is why the long holds are discouraged immediately before competition. | warm-up, temperature, stretch, prime, pattern, force | what is a warm-up, should you stretch before exercise, why not stretch before sprinting | warm-up | warm-up
